  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ELEPHANT CONSERVATION AND MONITORING IN THE LEUSER ECOSYSTEM, SUMATRA, INDONESIA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE CRITICALLY ENDANGERED SUMATRAN ELEPHANT CONTINUES TO FACE PRESSURE FROM HABITAT DESTRUCTION, THE RESULTING FREQUENTLY ESCALATING HUMAN-ELEPHANT CONFLICT (HEC), AND POACHING. THERE ARE MOST LIKELY FEWER THAN 1,500 SUMATRAN ELEPHANTS LEFT IN THE WILD, SCATTERED ACROSS THE ISLAND IN SMALL AND ISOLATED SUBPOPULATIONS. MANY OF THESE SURVIVING SUBPOPULATIONS ARE TOO SMALL TO BE GENETICALLY VIABLE AND THE UNAVAILABILITY OF ADDITIONAL SUITABLE HABITAT AND SEVERE HEC REDUCES THE CHANCES OF INCREASING OR EVEN MAINTAINING THEIR NUMBERS IN MOST AREAS. THIS PROJECT AIMS AT PROTECTING SUMATRAN ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITAT IN THE LEUSER ECOSYSTEM, THE ONLY PLACE ON EARTH WHERE ELEPHANTS, TIGERS, ORANGUTANS AND RHINOS ARE FOUND IN THE SAME LANDSCAPE. HOME TO ONE OF THE LARGEST ELEPHANT SUBPOPULATIONS - AND WITH ABOUT 2.6 MILLION HECTARES AMONG THE LARGEST REMAINING RAINFORESTS IN SUMATRA - THE LEUSER ECOSYSTEM IS A KEY AREA FOR ELEPHANT SURVIVAL IN INDONESIA. THREE ELEPHANT PROTECTION TEAMS (EPTS) WILL IMPLEMENT REGULAR PATROLS TO MONITOR AND PREVENT POACHING AND ENCROACHMENT IN THE PROJECT AREA, AND WORK CLOSELY WITH LOCAL FARMING COMMUNITIES TO MITIGATE HEC. THE WORK OF THE EPTS WILL BE SUPPORTED BY A NEW BIOACOUSTIC ELEPHANT MONITORING SYSTEM, THE ELOC, THAT WE PLAN TO FURTHER DEVELOP AND EVALUATE AGAINST ESTABLISHED MONI</narrative>
  </description>
